#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f2cc01-7206-5396-aeef-8e4a73809186" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving Two Unknown Corresponding Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "142170e2-0b2d-5612-92d8-7c989aa1453e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10736" :question/problem "![](images/30/30081d2bd598ed395c5f9ac372b0d9899cc3e6f7ba0a26fefb27a2729b3dbef3.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $\\angle{AJF}$ and $\\angle{CIF}$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AJF & =m∠CIF \\\\ 9x-{10}^{∘} & =7x+{4}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-{10}^{∘} & =7x+{4}^{∘} \\\\ 9x-7x & ={4}^{∘}+{10}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={14}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{{14}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & ={7}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={7}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068450 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"}] :question/id #uuid "40e3c6c8-c86a-55bf-80ef-d8845828ad27" :question/math-academy-id "e-10855" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1d1410979b84b183e64f0537af77acde9cba2fabd14cf66eb90b33c4a388649.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m\\angle{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ca/cab8ca51722f9b2079db0f4c81fc0f89f26e23f99430ffac7ba709d945b6b08e.png)\n\nNote that $\\angle 2$ and the angle whose measure is $100^\\circ$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle 2 = 100^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $\\angle 3$ and the angle whose measure is $50^\\circ$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle 3 = 50^\\circ.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/21/215845989e689c4a479101a1e4f6815e884f86c328b0991a02a18e434640aceb.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle 1$, $\\angle 2,$ and $\\angle 3$ combine to give a straight angle. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{100}^{∘}+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{150}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068452 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85cc8613-7372-5e19-be2d-afd8d58d4708" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving an Unknown Corresponding Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "b983a05e-715b-5541-be19-cefac6f82b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11054" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c49d6d86122df4b39745e6e917c38b50efbeb7b5209d1a24e3b8eb19459f2326.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $\\angle{CIF}$ and $\\angle{AJI}$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠CIF & =m∠AJI \\\\ 5x+{10}^{∘} & ={110}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+{10}^{∘} & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={100}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{{100}^{∘}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & ={20}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={20}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cb30b2e-2f71-55a0-a80a-f7a0f5e71780" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals That Require Solving an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "c7d91325-bc8d-571d-8ae7-aa66ae0534c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11055" :question/problem "![](images/60/606635acf3efd4f00a82cbc849d11bc6c67543357c84bb37344c9836a2c249e0.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $s \\parallel t.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/23/23f64f62e4a78ccc60e1f2461dc9962f0eac00edac475305c9550419afe956bd.png)\n\nNote that $\\angle 1$ and the angle whose measure is $x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle 1 = x.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $\\angle 2$ and the angle whose measure is $2x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm\\angle 2 = 2x.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram.\n\n![](images/74/74448c1e414d472be9e80c8a78e5517f0c431e5e7dde5e21bf3972e137af529c.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle 1$, $\\angle 2,$ and the third angle whose measure is $45^\\circ$, combine to give a straight angle. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+{45}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+2x+{45}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{45}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={135}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{{135}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1222347a-9c45-51e7-8322-bcf3061f61eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497841} {:answer/id #uuid "52344484-529a-5849-8319-c2d0528f3202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497994} {:answer/id #uuid "b76d9d5c-8920-5995-8b79-d91f23f47434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498208} {:answer/id #uuid "c665d3d4-6cc4-53d9-8415-c786bbbe5100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498239} {:answer/id #uuid "de36d2ad-2fb6-5d01-80fb-106c7444b15d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76d9d5c-8920-5995-8b79-d91f23f47434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498208} :answer-field/id #uuid "c00ac977-2a8a-5caf-acf7-c78b1a988db1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d5fd621-5efb-551e-9918-9da1db5f61d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-155553" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a661c340c9eb953b93ceeebb23cd3d251a5591db3893a917f94f465c81bbffc.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/db/db08637378dcf3968cb0e526057212d7c673fb1818eb84db5f076eb9cf4fb2c9.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${110}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={110}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${45}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/94/9420706692d49a3faf575d20be6b64f306d9fc901e477184e6b2f2ad21907d8f.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combined give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{110}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{155}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cb30b2e-2f71-55a0-a80a-f7a0f5e71780" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals That Require Solving an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0253ad1f-e4e4-5656-988f-419f1e772e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{17}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497803} {:answer/id #uuid "102afb52-f12e-5718-82ab-ebff01b67c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497836} {:answer/id #uuid "17b135ca-a28e-5dec-92f0-b693bf9ecf45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497854} {:answer/id #uuid "74901d77-0eea-54e4-8a01-a777cd91957a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498077} {:answer/id #uuid "c216fb2f-399f-57e6-a58c-f073fe668966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b135ca-a28e-5dec-92f0-b693bf9ecf45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497854} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ded0af1-d490-5f2b-9a4f-c22bcd03816c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a255fe3a-27d6-5e10-9557-556a8eb2f192" :question/math-academy-id "q-155558" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c52160535ccb69dd4a70c6e8a172529b599a13e45e3828be7be03da95e35c10c.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $s∥t.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠1$ and $∠2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd9f8d0ecb9bf35fa0a3c8f478a2f25062d37013704d4e1b4e3b0de27c2b91cc.png)\n\nNote that $∠1$ and the angle whose measure is $2x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠1=2x.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is $5x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2=5x.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram.\n\n![](images/fe/fe578ba5253792e812decdab76c76afd31e1d3e2f709bd202b1b0ecda5ef3fc4.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and the third angle whose measure is ${40}^{∘}$, combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+5x+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{{140}^{∘}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & ={20}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={20}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85cc8613-7372-5e19-be2d-afd8d58d4708" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving an Unknown Corresponding Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f4f19f2-764d-5714-85bf-78a36dff2c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497867} {:answer/id #uuid "69dd7da9-81f7-543c-ad45-812af78f6b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498054} {:answer/id #uuid "881f827c-af81-50fc-8fd7-e8d18bf86d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498112} {:answer/id #uuid "b375db35-1491-551a-ba0d-73d83631845b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498199} {:answer/id #uuid "c63441ed-2431-5704-b48f-8fd7b72f8e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b375db35-1491-551a-ba0d-73d83631845b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498199} :answer-field/id #uuid "683884ff-3eb2-51c9-8371-65622365d230" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ee3599c-3202-5bd4-938b-9fc71740af88" :question/math-academy-id "q-158608" :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebe7727862c160f7684d07ce6474ce0d57a95b6963415a1c3fc651aa2f951fdb.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $∠AJI$ and $∠CIF$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AJI & =m∠CIF \\\\ 3x & ={75}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={75}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{{75}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} & ={25}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a95a80d-bce2-5fef-a0df-eec0aca71e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{47}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497823} {:answer/id #uuid "48a7ab9a-6ab7-512e-8f54-3e945af3a890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497965} {:answer/id #uuid "9e653195-8d58-5494-9216-89af4c60fd81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{51}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498154} {:answer/id #uuid "d65e0480-e0d6-5305-81c9-3a697459afb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498280} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bdbb51-9a8f-5c3f-a702-57842b93056d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48a7ab9a-6ab7-512e-8f54-3e945af3a890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497965} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb8109d3-8648-5df5-bb5c-781b19da41ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853e9125-a8ad-54ff-806e-0eedd0926871" :question/math-academy-id "q-158620" :question/problem "![](images/05/050b3e1f5370933abecefb4427ce2a3a747fc178ce68ca4849aebb53643e9119.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/54/54294a339432476e3b426a8a4bbeca3e4a6f3d2bb3f6cd662f98dd0d5117a248.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${97}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={97}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${38}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={38}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/40/4042c15df28fd0d4055d95647fada0ad2ffc966a1561b7a021e1dfa62ed5d5ba.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combined give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{97}^{∘}+{38}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{135}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cb30b2e-2f71-55a0-a80a-f7a0f5e71780" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals That Require Solving an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c148d24-a696-535d-ae17-61b04c7931cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498019} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8904d8-1d51-5705-b391-be32af0b2294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498064} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cae9ec-64b1-54d0-8073-f0c56278cb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498174} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1a35ca-f568-5a28-98ab-28a674f4b1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498346} {:answer/id #uuid "fce0a585-a80b-5b6b-89e4-1a124ea2ce5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c8904d8-1d51-5705-b391-be32af0b2294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498064} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7fb5959-aa21-53d0-9a51-e0b8efc1b111" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a40cc6f-032a-5398-9f26-b713251fc24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-158730" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4bd77dce6f600ed55fd3443cca91ae3f95b052e38d451334decc0f0b85dd94e.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $s∥t.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠1$ and $∠2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/25/259f366823da9de4543e0e245ec1186ac8f50e1a53616290e76271f9672a92f4.png)\n\nNote that $∠1$ and the angle whose measure is $2x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠1=2x.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is $5x+{5}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2=5x+{5}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/10629276463e5cf33b4a0c5b0ba1237eec1201fe142e644628f0a6a96fa54c32.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and the third angle whose measure is ${70}^{∘}$, combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+(5x+{5}^{∘})+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={105}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{{105}^{∘}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & ={15}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85cc8613-7372-5e19-be2d-afd8d58d4708" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving an Unknown Corresponding Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce93913-24ee-5b5b-9a9c-6ec5e8fea480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497863} {:answer/id #uuid "2065ac5a-d13c-5b11-9810-160ceb89a4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497868} {:answer/id #uuid "4130da62-5488-5512-b60f-c61812e1f56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497944} {:answer/id #uuid "45425d8b-b58b-5638-9bff-b966a3b920c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497954} {:answer/id #uuid "97eccd48-7021-5a23-b4a3-17d2fff74915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ce93913-24ee-5b5b-9a9c-6ec5e8fea480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497863} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff03f103-fbbc-566d-8f3a-c5a0ed23f5dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4e65be3-24c7-5463-8001-08ac073feca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-4170" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c89ac7325d47cb580e1479a90be32f923df0f17f8c478ad7265d135f4b1f4738.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $∠FID$ and $∠IJB$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠FID & =m∠IJB \\\\ {60}^{∘} & =8x-{4}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{60}^{∘} & =8x-{4}^{∘} \\\\ {64}^{∘} & =8x \\\\ \\frac{{64}^{∘}}{8} & =\\frac{8x}{8} \\\\ {8}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}x}{\\cancel{8}} \\\\ {8}^{∘} & =x \\\\ x & ={8}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f2cc01-7206-5396-aeef-8e4a73809186" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving Two Unknown Corresponding Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da8e4c3-a2ff-579e-933b-4967e716c92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497831} {:answer/id #uuid "1d704696-8398-565f-bb5f-b7a623613594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497865} {:answer/id #uuid "3bba9cef-cf0b-5043-afc8-9269900ee53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497934} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6ec853-bcd9-584f-b9c8-1e70a67f4a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497936} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc2f690-2637-564a-92dc-f01efe7404f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bc2f690-2637-564a-92dc-f01efe7404f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498016} :answer-field/id #uuid "510990da-b574-5475-ad10-2eaa55e106eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a557528-702d-5b20-982a-c15a6617584f" :question/math-academy-id "q-53163" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e0fe451cf2c2989a1382cdc47626d683da2918649afc12c593d5105640bd730.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $∠AJI$ and $∠CIF$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AJI & =m∠CIF \\\\ x+{23}^{∘} & =2x-{1}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{23}^{∘} & =2x-{1}^{∘} \\\\ {23}^{∘} & =x-{1}^{∘} \\\\ {24}^{∘} & =x \\\\ x & ={24}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f2cc01-7206-5396-aeef-8e4a73809186" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving Two Unknown Corresponding Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d475d8-5700-564a-8131-473fe88b21e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497929} {:answer/id #uuid "415f4997-545f-5d3a-9607-905ac0e56247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497946} {:answer/id #uuid "5686d8f0-8257-5365-b6c1-cebdfd074a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498004} {:answer/id #uuid "adae88f6-414b-57df-80f1-3ab0749d4f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498185} {:answer/id #uuid "cc58358d-3e76-5b43-832b-37efa1fc307d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39d475d8-5700-564a-8131-473fe88b21e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497929} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7ac5d8-9151-5355-946d-7b5a16fa5062" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "720d6f86-e4a5-558d-9fc4-14221a99ea96" :question/math-academy-id "q-67783" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc14287b5440a8d3afc5a647a77e18d7891843bb4a4c21b62f011427ffd7bc58.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $∠EIC$ and $∠IJA$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠EIC & =m∠IJA \\\\ 3x-{23}^{∘} & =2x+{31}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{23}^{∘} & =2x+{31}^{∘} \\\\ x-{23}^{∘} & ={31}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={54}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
